<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Analysis settings for the Sonar scan, passed with /s: from .github/workflows/sonar-analysis.yml.

  This file rather than sonar-project.properties because the Scanner for .NET does not read that one - it
  ignores it silently, with no warning. This xml is its equivalent. The /s: path must be absolute.

  Token and host url stay on the command line. The token is a secret and does not belong in a committed file.

  There is deliberately no sonar.inclusions. The scanner builds its module list from the build itself, so a
  source glob can only fight it: an inclusion of `src/**/*` in the SonarCloud UI, left over from a flat
  layout, is what made the 2026-08-07 run index 0 files and still report success. Scope is exclusions only.
-->
<SonarQubeAnalysisProperties xmlns="http://www.sonarsource.com/msbuild/integration/2015/1">

  <!-- C# reaches the scanner through the build. TS reaches it through multi-language analysis, which walks
       the working tree instead, so this list is mostly about keeping that walk off generated and vendored
       files - node_modules alone was most of the 15794 files the broken run looked at.

       docs/ and web/ are the two published Jekyll sites. They are excluded because they are a separate
       deliverable with their own session, not because their code does not matter. Drop them from this line
       if you want the ~30 hand-written files under docs/_js and web/_js scanned too.

       assets/lib/ is vendored: alpine, beercss, material-dynamic-colors and swagger-ui, shipped as-is. It was
       8157 of the 50418 ncloc the 2026-08-08 run measured and 274 of its 1083 issues - 227 in alpine.js alone,
       including the only CRITICAL bug. None of it is ours to fix, and all 2329 of its lines counted as
       uncovered, which is where most of the missing coverage came from.

       shared/data/ is the test fixture corpus (bischoff-suite, or-library, custom-problems, result-selection).
       It was the whole json=14010 slice of that same run - 28% of the project measured as data files. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.exclusions">**/node_modules/**,**/bin/**,**/obj/**,build/**,results/**,docs/**,web/**,assets/lib/**,shared/data/**</Property>

  <!-- One flat file per suite in build/coverage/sonar, split by extension: .xml from the C# collector,
       .info from jest. config/tests.just is what writes them and the only place that knows the suite list. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.cs.vscoveragexml.reportsPaths">build/coverage/sonar/*.xml</Property>
  <Property Name="sonar.javascript.lcov.reportPaths">build/coverage/sonar/*.info</Property>

  <!-- The v1/v2 variants of First/Best/Worst Fit Decreasing are separate implementations that happen to share
       a shape, not copy-paste waiting to be factored out. Sonar cannot tell the difference and read them at
       93% duplicated, which was most of the 38% it reported for lib/ and the reason the gate failed on
       duplication. Detection is off for that folder only, so duplication elsewhere in lib/ still counts.

       This is a duplication exclusion, not an issue ignore: every rule still runs on these files. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.cpd.exclusions">lib/src/Binacle.Lib/Algorithms/**</Property>

  <!-- Build tooling, not shipped code. The .NET support projects (benchmarks, performance suites, tools and
       the two test kernels) are handled properly instead, by SonarQubeTestProject in Directory.Build.props -
       that takes them out of the coverage denominator and off the product rule set in one move. -->
  <Property Name="sonar.coverage.exclusions">gulpfile.js,jest.config.js</Property>

  <!-- No sonar.issue.ignore rules here, on purpose. Where a rule was wrong about our code the code now says
       so in a way the analyser can read - [AssertionMethod] on the helpers that assert, arrange/act/assert
       split so the check sits in the test body - rather than the finding being hidden from this file, where
       nobody reviewing the code would ever see it. If a rule genuinely does not apply to this project, turn
       it off in the quality profile, where it is visible to everyone, not here. -->

</SonarQubeAnalysisProperties>
